Router needs the Runtime to create the signal. `Router::new(runtime, initial)`. Or the app creates a `WritableSignal<String>` and passes it. Let me make `Router` a lightweight struct holding a `WritableSignal<String>` (current path) + a Vec back-stack + a RouteTable.

```rust
pub struct Router {
    path: WritableSignal<String>,
    states: Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>,  // back-stack
    table: Rc<RouteTable>,  // maybe
}
impl Router {
    pub fn new(path: WritableSignal<String>) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn path(&self) -> String { self.path.get().unwrap_or_default() }
    pub fn navigate(&self, to: impl Into<String>) { self.path.set(to.into()); }
    pub fn push(&self, to: impl Into<String>) { self.states.borrow_mut().push(self.path()); self.path.set(to.into()); }
    pub fn pop(&self) { if let Some(p) = self.states.borrow_mut().pop() { self.path.set(p); } }
