Here's what a simple audio player would look like under the direct-reference model (no scoping). It mirrors the existing demo's API.

## The controller — a state object the app holds

```java
import dev.pathland.view.media.MediaController;
import dev.pathland.view.signal.Signals;

// Created wherever the app owns playback state (a field, not a View).
MediaController player = new MediaController();
player.source("/_pathland/assets/audio/track1.mp3");   // or player.source(someSignal)

// Command signals (the app / user writes these):
player.playing();     // WritableSignal<Boolean>
player.position();    // WritableSignal<Float>  — a write = seek
player.volume();      // WritableSignal<Float>
player.muted();       // WritableSignal<Boolean>
player.loop();        // WritableSignal<Boolean>
player.rate();        // WritableSignal<Float>

// Report signals (the emitter fills these from MEDIA_* events — never commands):
player.time();        // Signal<Float>  — MEDIA_TIME_UPDATED
player.duration();    // Signal<Float>  — MEDIA_DURATION_CHANGED
player.volumeReport();// Signal<Float>  — MEDIA_VOLUME_CHANGED
player.onEnded(() -> nextTrack());

// Convenience (SwiftUI AVPlayer-style):
player.play();  player.pause();  player.toggle();
player.seek(12.5f);  player.setVolume(0.7f);
```

## The view — controls bind the controller directly

```java
public final class AudioPlayer implements View, Configurable<AudioPlayer.Config> {

    public static final class Config implements View.Config {
        private MediaController player;             // referenced directly — no scoping

        public Config player(MediaController player) { this.player = player; return this; }
    }

    private final Config config;
    private AudioPlayer(Config config) { this.config = config; }

    public static ViewBuilder<AudioPlayer, Config> make(Consumer<Config> configure) {
        Config config = new Config();
        configure.accept(config);
        return new ViewBuilder<>(new AudioPlayer(config));
    }

    @Override public Config config() { return config; }

    @Override public View body() {
        MediaController p = config.player;

        // Play/pause: label is reactive, action writes the command signal.
        var playButton = Button.make(b -> b.action(p::toggle))
                .children(Text.make(computed(() -> p.playing().get() ? "Pause" : "Play")));

        // Seek: the slider DISPLAYS the report, and its drags write the command.
        var seek = Slider.make(s -> s
                        .value(p.time())            // display: MEDIA_TIME_UPDATED
                        .in(0f, 1f)                 // 0..duration via a PercentSeek wrapper, as today
                        .onChange(p::seek));        // command: MEDIA_POSITION

        var volume = Slider.make(s -> s.value(p.volume()).in(0f, 1f));

        return VStack.make(v -> v.alignment(HorizontalAlignment.CENTER).spacing(12))
                .children(playButton, seek, volume);
    }
}
```

## What the emitter does invisibly

The author never writes a node id. Because `player` is referenced by the view, the emitter:
1. collects the distinct `MediaController` instances,
2. emits one `MEDIA_CONTROLLER` node each (non-visual, at the root),
3. binds `playing/position/volume/…` as its property bindings (so `player.toggle()` emits only a `PLAYBACK_STATE` delta),
4. writes `MEDIA_CONTROLLER_ID` on any `Video` surface referencing it,
5. routes `MEDIA_*` reports back into `time()/duration()/volumeReport()`.

## The SizeThatFits case (same `player`, two branches)

```java
VStack.make()
    .children(
        SizeThatFits.make(
            Fit.of(PlayerBar.make(b -> b.player(player)), 700),   // compact
            Fit.of(NowPlayingBar.make(b -> b.player(player))))     // wide
    );
```

Both branches get the **same** `player`, so a band swap re-emits only the controls; the controller node (and its playback) is untouched — which is the whole point.

One thing worth confirming: the seek slider above uses a `PercentSeek`-style wrapper (percent ↔ seconds, since the renderer works in seconds) — I'd keep that helper as-is rather than make `Slider` duration-aware. Want me to also sketch the `Video` surface + the emitter diff, or is this enough to lock the DSL shape?

